,title,link,text,language,family
0,Dhuwal language - Wikipedia,https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dhuwal_language,"Dhuwaya is a stigmatised contact variant [clarification needed] used by the younger generation in informal contexts, and is the form taught in schools, having replaced Gumatj ca. 1990. [citation needed]",Dhuwaya,Australian
1,Dhuwaya - Sorosoro,https://www.sorosoro.org/en/dhuwaya/,"Dhuwaya has no clan affiliations and no associated territory, although it is associated in a non-traditional way with the Yirrkala settlement. Number of speakers: The 2005 National Indigenous Languages Survey Report estimated approximately 1500 speakers of Dhuwaya, whereas the 2006 Australian Bureau of Statistics Census counted 326 speakers.",Dhuwaya,Australian
2,"Dhuwaya language. Evangelism tools, church planting resources ...",https://globalrecordings.net/en/language/dwy,"Dhuwaya language and dialect information. Audio Bible stories and lessons. Download free evangelism resources, MP3s, audio bible study tools, language/dialect information.",Dhuwaya,Australian
3,Dhuwaya and Yolngu Matha: Living Languages of Arnhem Land,https://fatsil.org/australian-indigenous-languages/yolngu-matha-2/,"Dhuwaya and Yolngu Matha serve as essential vessels for passing down stories, beliefs, and customs. By speaking these languages, you keep rituals alive, strengthen social bonds, and guarantee future generations understand their heritage. Your use of these languages sustains the rich cultural fabric of Arnhem Land, fostering pride and continuity.",Dhuwaya,Australian
4,"An Australian koine: Dhuwaya, a variety of Yolŋu Matha spoken at ...",https://www.degruyterbrill.com/document/doi/10.1515/ijsl.1993.99.45/html,"Article An Australian koine: Dhuwaya, a variety of Yolŋu Matha spoken at Yirrkala in Worth East Arnhemland was published on January 1, 1993 in the journal International Journal of the Sociology of Language (volume 1993, issue 99).",Dhuwaya,Australian
5,Dhuwal language explained,https://everything.explained.today/Dhuwaya/,"Dhuwal (also Dual, Duala) is one of the Yolŋu languages spoken by Aboriginal Australians in the Northern Territory, Australia. Although all Yolŋu languages are mutually intelligible to some extent, Dhuwal represents a distinct dialect continuum of eight separate varieties. In 2019, Djambarrpuyŋu became the first Indigenous language to be spoken in an Australian parliament, when Yolŋu man ...",Dhuwaya,Australian
6,Territory Stories - Manikaymi Djorra',https://territorystories.nt.gov.au/10070/870947,"Provenance This material was collected by the Living Archive of Aboriginal Languages Project between 2013 and 2021. The project was led by Charles Darwin University in partnership with the Australian National University and the Northern Territory Government and funded in part by the Australian Research Council.,",Dhuwaya,Australian
7,AIATSIS AustLang Online,https://aiatsis.gov.au/austlang/language/N116,"Dhuwaya is a common contemporary dialect of Yolngu which has arisen from language contact. It is also known as Baby Gumatj (N116). Thus, unlike other Yolngu dialects, Dhuwaya has no clan affiliations and no associated territory, although it is associated in a non-traditional way with the Yirrkala settlement. It is used universally by the younger generation in all informal contexts, but ...",Dhuwaya,Australian
8,A new diglossia : contemporary speech varieties at Yirrkala in North ...,https://openresearch-repository.anu.edu.au/items/4b647a29-c8f8-4097-b955-bcc0191a09e3,"As a Balanda (white Australian) without having previous exposure to Top End Northern Territory communities or to Aboriginal languages of N.E. Arnhem Land3, data collection and transcription proved extremely difficult. This was especially the case because Dhuwaya is a highly stigmatized language variety at Yirrkala.",Dhuwaya,Australian
